WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.300
Movie

00:01.300 --> 00:03.100
Elements in action

00:03.100 --> 00:05.000
Have you ever wondered how a match lights up

00:05.000 --> 00:08.300
or why an apple turns brown when bitten and left out?

00:08.300 --> 00:11.400
The magic of chemistry is at play in these everyday phenomena

00:11.400 --> 00:14.500
and countless others we often take for granted.

00:14.500 --> 00:17.300
It's a silent symphony of atoms and molecules

00:17.300 --> 00:20.000
dancing to the beat of nature's laws.

00:20.000 --> 00:22.400
It's a world where matter and energy intertwine

00:22.400 --> 00:25.800
in an endless ballet of reactions and transformations.

00:25.800 --> 00:29.600
In this video, you will discover the answers to these questions and more

00:29.700 --> 00:32.800
as we delve into the fascinating world of chemistry.

00:32.800 --> 00:34.300
Imagine striking a match.

00:34.300 --> 00:38.200
The sudden burst of flame is not magic, it's chemistry in action.

00:38.200 --> 00:41.800
When you strike a match, a chemical reaction known as combustion occurs.

00:41.800 --> 00:44.500
This reaction is not as complicated as it may seem.

00:44.500 --> 00:49.400
It involves just two main players, phosphorus and oxygen.

00:49.400 --> 00:51.600
As the match scrapes against the rough surface,

00:51.600 --> 00:55.600
the friction generates enough heat to ignite the phosphorus on the match head.

00:55.700 --> 00:59.800
This is where our second player, the oxygen in the air, steps in.

00:59.800 --> 01:02.300
Oxygen teams up with the burning phosphorus

01:02.300 --> 01:04.900
and a chemical reaction takes place.

01:04.900 --> 01:09.200
This reaction is exothermic, meaning it gives off heat and that's what creates the flame.

01:09.200 --> 01:12.500
It also produces light, another by-product of combustion.

01:12.500 --> 01:14.600
But here's the fascinating part.

01:14.600 --> 01:20.000
Every single flame you see is a testament to this incredible chemical reaction.

01:20.000 --> 01:23.200
From the tiny flame of a match to the roaring bonfire,

01:23.200 --> 01:28.400
it's all down to the simple yet powerful process of combustion.

01:28.400 --> 01:33.800
So the next time you strike a match, remember it's not just a flame, it's a chemical reaction.

01:33.800 --> 01:36.300
Now, let's take a bite of an apple and leave it out.

01:36.300 --> 01:39.300
Ever noticed how it turns brown?

01:39.300 --> 01:43.400
This seemingly mundane phenomenon is actually a fascinating dance of molecules,

01:43.400 --> 01:45.600
a process known as oxidation.

01:45.600 --> 01:48.200
You see, within the apple, there exist enzymes,

01:48.200 --> 01:51.900
biological catalysts that speed up chemical reactions.

01:51.900 --> 01:54.800
One particular enzyme, called polyphenol oxidase,

01:54.800 --> 01:58.100
springs into action when the apple is cut or bitten into.

01:58.100 --> 02:02.500
This exposure to oxygen triggers the enzyme to transform certain compounds in the apple,

02:02.500 --> 02:07.300
known as phenols, into a different compound called okinones.

02:07.300 --> 02:10.500
These okinones then react further, ultimately producing melanin,

02:10.500 --> 02:13.900
the very pigment that gives the apple its brown hue.

02:13.900 --> 02:15.500
But wait, there's more.

02:15.500 --> 02:20.500
This process is not exclusive to apples, it occurs in various fruits and vegetables like pears and potatoes.

02:20.500 --> 02:23.100
So the next time you witness your apple turning brown,

02:23.100 --> 02:26.600
remember it's not magic, it's not decay, it's chemistry.

02:26.600 --> 02:32.400
So an apple turning brown is not just a natural process, it's an example of chemistry in action.

02:32.400 --> 02:36.400
From the striking of a match to the browning of an apple, chemistry is everywhere.

02:36.400 --> 02:42.600
It's in the vibrant hues of a sunset, the sizzle of a steak, even the fizz in your soda.

02:42.600 --> 02:47.200
It's in the warmth of a candle flame, the sweet scent of an apple pie.

02:47.200 --> 02:53.000
We've explored combustion, unraveled the apple enigma, and reveled in the magic of chemistry.

02:53.000 --> 02:57.700
Remember, every time you see a flame or a browning apple, it's not just nature, it's chemistry in action.

